---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "Best Smart Home Gym (2026): 5 Picks and Their Real Costs";
const description =
  "Five smart home gyms sold on Amazon, checked against makers and reviews: Tonal, Speediance and SQUATZ. Digital resistance, wall vs freestanding, memberships.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "tonal-2",
    name: "Tonal 2",
    brand: "Tonal",
    category: "Wall-mounted digital-resistance smart gym",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0F8FD523V?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41ip4D6LVsL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$4,295",
    priceBounds: {"low":4295,"high":4295},
    specs: "5–250 lb digital resistance (125 lb per arm) · 50.9 x 21.5 in (H x W) · wall-mounted to studs, professional installation · needs 7 ft of wall, 7 x 7 ft of floor, 7 ft 10 in ceiling ideal · 2-year warranty",
    pros: [
      "The most complete guided-training experience here, with camera-based form feedback",
      "Hangs on the wall, so it takes almost no floor space when not in use",
      "Load set by the machine from 5 to 250 lb, with no plates or pins",
      "Installed by Tonal's own team onto wood or metal studs 16 to 24 inches apart",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Requires a 12-month membership commitment at $59.95 a month, and most features need an active membership",
      "Smart Accessories ($495 or $595) and delivery and installation (from $295) are extra",
      "A 250 lb total, split between two arms, will not satisfy strong lifters on heavy compound lifts",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For someone who wants a coach on the wall and a clear floor, and who will use the membership. Not for heavy lifters or anyone who cannot drill.",
    description: "Tonal 2 is a wall-mounted unit with two adjustable arms and motors that produce resistance from 5 to 250 lb, 125 lb per arm, figures Tonal and Garage Gym Reviews agree on. Tonal's comparison table adds that coaching cues come through its built-in camera plus your phone for a side angle, a feature Garage Gym Reviews also describes. Tonal installs the unit itself: its installation page says it mounts much like a TV bracket on most wood and metal studs 16 to 24 inches apart, needs a grounded three-prong outlet within 6 feet and steady Wi-Fi, and works best with 7 ft 10 in of ceiling, though it can work with 7 ft. Our earlier check of Tonal and Garage Gym Reviews agreed on 7 ft of wall and a 7 x 7 ft floor area, and on the 50.9 x 21.5 in body. On 19 October Tonal listed the hardware at $4,295, with delivery and installation from $295, Smart Accessories bundles at $495 and $595 that it says are required for full functionality, and the membership at $59.95 a month with a 12-month commitment. An Amazon listing showed a higher price that day, so compare. The warranty is two years, with paid extensions.",
  },
  {
    id: "speediance-gym-monster-2",
    name: "Speediance Gym Monster 2",
    brand: "Speediance",
    category: "Freestanding, folding digital-resistance smart gym",
    badge: "Best Freestanding",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0DFH1NYR6?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41sgv13FSlL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$3,387–$3,999",
    priceBounds: {"low":3387,"high":3999},
    specs: "220 lb maximum (110 lb per side) · about 48 x 27 x 73 in unfolded, about 14.5 in deep folded · about 172 lb · freestanding, no drilling · 2-year main-unit warranty",
    pros: [
      "No drilling or wall mounting: the obvious digital choice for renters",
      "Folds to about 14.5 in deep against a wall when you are done",
      "Works without the optional subscription for basic strength training, PCWorld reports",
      "Packages add benches and a rowing attachment, so one purchase can cover more movements",
    ],
    cons: [
      "About 172 lb, delivered in heavy packages; plan for help or pay for in-home delivery",
      "220 lb in total, 110 lb per side, is a ceiling strong lifters will reach",
      "Some functions need the paid Wellness+ subscription, and its price has varied between sources",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The best smart gym for a home where you cannot or will not drill, and the cheapest digital machine here to keep running.",
    description: "The Gym Monster 2 is a freestanding digital machine: two motors produce up to 220 lb, 110 lb per side, figures Speediance and PCWorld agree on. Speediance and PCWorld put it at about 48 in deep, 27 in wide and 73 in tall unfolded and about 14.5 in deep folded, and about 172 lb. Speediance says it needs no drilling or wall mounting, and it folds for storage. PCWorld describes three packages totalling just under 353 lb, and Speediance sells in-home delivery separately from $150. On 19 October Speediance's store priced the Gym Monster 2 from $3,387 for the Basic package to $3,999 for Family Plus, with Works and Works Plus between, and Amazon listings showed the same span. Wellness+ is optional: Speediance sold it at $19.99 a month or $149.99 a year that day, while Garage Gym Reviews' roundup, which covers the original Gym Monster, lists $29 a month, so check at checkout; PCWorld says some functions require it. The main unit carries a two-year warranty, with paid extensions. Speediance's newer Gym Monster 3 sits above it at $4,099 and up.",
  },
  {
    id: "speediance-gym-monster-refurbished",
    name: "Speediance Gym Monster (Refurbished)",
    brand: "Speediance",
    category: "Refurbished freestanding digital-resistance smart gym",
    badge: "Best Value Smart Gym",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CHYDNKNK?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31Icbsv0oUL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $1,999 refurbished (Amazon, 9 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":1999,"high":1999},
    specs: "220 lb maximum (110 lb per side) · about 187 lb · folds against a wall · freestanding, no drilling · refurbished; check the listing for warranty terms",
    pros: [
      "Digital resistance up to 220 lb for well under half the price of a new Tonal 2",
      "Freestanding and folding, so no studs, drilling or installation visit",
      "Sold on Amazon, which none of our earlier value picks were",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The first-generation machine: older than the Gym Monster 2 above, and refurbished",
      "The listing does not make the refurbished unit’s warranty clear to us; confirm it before buying",
      "Some features sit behind Speediance’s paid subscription, and its price has differed between sources",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For someone who wants a digital machine for the least money and can live with a refurbished, first-generation unit. Confirm the warranty first.",
    description: "This card replaces the Tonal 1 Certified Refurbished, which Tonal sells only from its own store and which we could not find on Amazon. The closest Amazon equivalent, a refurbished first-generation smart gym, is Speediance's original Gym Monster, listed on Amazon at about $1,999 on 9 November 2026. Garage Gym Reviews' spec table and T3 agree on a unit weight of about 187 lb and on 220 lb of resistance, 110 lb per side, the same maximum as the Gym Monster 2; Garage Gym Reviews gives the footprint as about 4.1 x 2.3 ft and 6 ft tall unfolded, folding to about 1.15 ft deep. Like the Gym Monster 2 it stands on its own base, so there is no wall to drill. Garage Gym Reviews' roundup lists a $29-a-month subscription for this model, and some functions need it; check the current price. New units carried a two-year warranty in the listings we saw, but the refurbished listing's own terms are what apply, so read them before you order. Against the Tonal 1 it replaces, it gives up Tonal's coaching library and wall mount but has a higher maximum and no mandatory membership.",
  },
  {
    id: "speediance-gym-nano",
    name: "Speediance Gym Nano",
    brand: "Speediance",
    category: "Portable, rack-mounted digital cable unit",
    badge: "Best Rack Add-On",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HC2JJF9S?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31dS1r9wpmL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$1,699–$4,398 by package (Speediance); from about $1,699 on Amazon (9 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":1699,"high":4398},
    specs: "Up to 220 lb on one cable, 1 lb steps · about 18 lb · 16.7 ft of cable · rechargeable battery · knob quick-release rack mount included · 2-year main-unit warranty (1 year on battery and cable)",
    pros: [
      "Turns a power rack you already own into a smart cable station",
      "Up to 220 lb on one cable and 16.7 ft of cable travel, more than double the Voltra I’s 8.5 ft",
      "Battery powered and about 18 lb, with a travel case in the base package",
      "A rack mount is included rather than sold separately",
    ],
    cons: [
      "New in 2026, with little independent long-term coverage yet",
      "Only one year of warranty cover on the battery and cable",
      "The dual-unit packages cost as much as a full freestanding smart gym",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For a lifter who already has a rack and wants cable work and data without giving up the barbell, bought on Amazon.",
    description: "This card replaces the Beyond Power Voltra I, which Beyond Power sells direct and which we could not find on Amazon. The nearest Amazon-sold equivalent is Speediance's Gym Nano, a battery-powered single-cable motor unit that clamps to a power rack. Speediance and Garage Gym Reviews agree on up to 220 lb of resistance and 16.7 ft of cable, and Speediance and AndroidGuys give the unit as about 18 lb; Speediance says the battery lasts up to 8 hours depending on load and use. Garage Gym Reviews gives the warranty as two years on the main unit and one year on the battery and cable, and the price as $1,699 to $4,398 by package; Speediance's store showed the same span, with a knob quick-release rack mount and travel case in every package and two-unit Dual packages at the top. Against the Voltra I, it has about double the cable length and costs less with a mount included; the two units' maximum resistance is in the same 200 to 230 lb region, depending on which source and Voltra version you read. Speediance's page does not describe a subscription for it, so check what the app charges before you buy.",
  },
  {
    id: "squatz-apollo-board-mini",
    name: "SQUATZ Apollo Board Mini",
    brand: "SQUATZ",
    category: "Compact floor-board digital cable trainer",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0DB69559S?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41REoCi2fWL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $800–$912 by colour (Amazon, 9 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":800,"high":912},
    specs: "Seller's listing claims only: up to 150 lb of digital cable resistance · compact, foldable board · free app · accessories included · no figure confirmed independently",
    pros: [
      "The cheapest digital-resistance trainer we could find on Amazon with a named brand and app",
      "A flat board you stand on, so it needs no wall, rack or drilling and stores away",
      "Close to Tempo Move’s starting price, which it replaces here",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Figures are the seller's listing claims; we could not confirm them independently",
      "150 lb is the lowest maximum on this page",
      "No camera form tracking and no real dumbbells, unlike the Tempo Move it replaces",
      "We found no independent review, warranty statement or long-term record",
    ],
    bottomLine: "A low-cost, small-space way into digital resistance, but buy it on the listing’s claims alone only if Amazon’s returns process covers it.",
    description: "This card replaces Tempo Move, which Tempo sells only from its own store and which we could not find on Amazon. We found no well-documented camera-tracked dumbbell system on Amazon (the best-known smart dumbbells there, BowFlex's SelectTech line, were recalled in 2025), so this slot now shows the best-reviewed budget digital trainer Amazon sells. The SQUATZ Apollo Board Mini is a board you stand on with motor-driven cables; its Amazon listing describes up to 150 lb of resistance, a compact folding design, accessories and a free app, at about $800 to $912 depending on colour on 9 November 2026. Figures are the seller's listing claims; we could not confirm them independently, and we found no independent review or warranty statement. We found no CPSC recall for SQUATZ. Against Tempo Move it costs about the same, takes less room and adds motorised resistance, but it gives up Tempo's real dumbbells, iPhone camera form feedback and coached programme.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Home Gym", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/home-gym/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Smart Home Gyms", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-smart-home-gyms/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best smart home gym?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most people who can wall-mount, the Tonal 2: 5 to 250 lb of digital resistance, camera form feedback and professional installation, at $4,295 plus a required $59.95-a-month membership. If you cannot drill, the freestanding Speediance Gym Monster 2 is the better choice, and its subscription is optional.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does a smart home gym cost per month?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On 19 October 2026, Tonal's membership was $59.95 a month with a 12-month commitment; Speediance's optional Wellness+ was $19.99 a month or $149.99 a year on its store; Garage Gym Reviews lists $29 a month for the original Gym Monster; and Speediance's Gym Nano page described no subscription. Prices change, so check before buying.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you use Tonal without a membership?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Only in a limited way. Tonal says each purchase requires a 12-month membership commitment and that an active membership is required for most features. If you want a digital machine that keeps most of its use without a subscription, the Speediance Gym Monster 2 or Gym Nano are better fits.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does a Tonal need to be mounted to studs?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Tonal says it installs much like a TV bracket on most wood and metal studs 16 to 24 inches apart, and its team does the installation. It also needs a grounded three-prong outlet within 6 feet, steady Wi-Fi, and ideally 7 feet 10 inches of ceiling, though Tonal says it can work with 7 feet.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best smart home gym for renters?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A freestanding or portable system. The Speediance Gym Monster 2 needs no drilling and folds against a wall, and the SQUATZ Apollo Board Mini is a small board you stand on. The Gym Nano needs a rack or mount rather than a wall. If you want a Tonal, get the landlord's written permission before installation.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a smart home gym worth it compared with weights?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is worth it if you will use the coaching and value the space saving and automatic load changes. It is poor value if you mainly want heavy squats and deadlifts, or will drop the membership after a few months. A rack, barbell and plates cost less over time and have no ceiling on load.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-19",
      dateModified: "2026-10-19",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      ...(product.brand ? { brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.brand } } : {}),
      ...(product.image ? { image: product.image.url } : {}),
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 5,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-smart-home-gyms/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/best-smart-home-gyms/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "home-gym");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const MORE = (GROUP?.pages ?? []).filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-19",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-19",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best smart home gym",
      "tonal 2",
      "speediance gym monster 2",
      "digital resistance home gym",
      "smart gym membership cost",
      "tonal alternative",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Best Overall: Tonal 2", link: PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/home-gym/">Home Gym</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Buying Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Best Smart Home Gym Picks, Wall Needs and Membership Costs</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        A smart home gym swaps plates and pins for motors or tracked free weights, a screen or phone app, and usually a monthly fee. Here are five currently sold systems, what each needs from your wall, floor and outlet, what the membership really adds over three years, and the products we left out on safety or availability grounds.
      </p>
      <p class="ed-answer"><strong>Short answer.</strong> Best Overall: <a href="#tonal-2">Tonal 2</a>. Best Freestanding: <a href="#speediance-gym-monster-2">Speediance Gym Monster 2</a>. Best Value Smart Gym: <a href="#speediance-gym-monster-refurbished">Speediance Gym Monster (Refurbished)</a>.</p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated October 19, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#top-picks" class="btn-ghost">View products</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <figure class="ed-hero">
        <div class="ed-hero-collage">
          <a href="#tonal-2"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41ip4D6LVsL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Tonal 2" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#speediance-gym-monster-2"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41sgv13FSlL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Speediance Gym Monster 2" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#speediance-gym-monster-refurbished"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31Icbsv0oUL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Speediance Gym Monster (Refurbished)" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Tonal 2 · Speediance Gym Monster 2 · Speediance Gym Monster (Refurbished)</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          A smart home gym promises a cable machine that sets its own weight, counts your reps and
          tells you what to do next, in less space than a rack of dumbbells. The best of them deliver
          most of that. They also ask more of the buyer than an ordinary machine: the right wall or a
          clear patch of floor, a grounded outlet and Wi-Fi, and in most cases a membership that
          outlives the excitement of the first month. The five systems below were chosen to cover the
          main designs — wall-mounted, freestanding, rack-mounted and camera-tracked free weights —
          with something you can buy today, and the guide after them is mostly about the costs and
          requirements the product pages put in small print.
        </p>

        <section id="top-picks" class="ed-toppicks not-prose">
          <h2>Our top picks:</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Overall:</span> <a href="#tonal-2">Tonal 2</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Freestanding:</span> <a href="#speediance-gym-monster-2">Speediance Gym Monster 2</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[1].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Value Smart Gym:</span> <a href="#speediance-gym-monster-refurbished">Speediance Gym Monster (Refurbished)</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[2].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Rack Add-On:</span> <a href="#speediance-gym-nano">Speediance Gym Nano</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[3].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Budget:</span> <a href="#squatz-apollo-board-mini">SQUATZ Apollo Board Mini</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[4].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the products below.</strong> We have not tested these
          systems, and no figure on this page is our own measurement. The Tonal 2 and Gym Monster 2 were
          confirmed as sold on the makers' stores on 19 October 2026, with resistance, dimensions,
          warranty and membership terms checked against at least one independent source — chiefly
          Garage Gym Reviews' smart home gym roundup and PCWorld's Speediance review via our cable
          machine guide. Our earlier picks for value, rack add-on and budget (the Tonal 1 Certified
          Refurbished, Beyond Power Voltra I and Tempo Move) are sold only by their makers, not on
          Amazon, so on 9 November 2026 we replaced their cards with the closest products Amazon sells:
          a refurbished original Speediance Gym Monster (checked against Garage Gym Reviews and T3), the
          Speediance Gym Nano (checked against Speediance and Garage Gym Reviews) and the SQUATZ Apollo
          Board Mini, whose figures are the seller's listing claims; we could not confirm them
          independently. A figure appears in a product's specification line only where the maker and an
          independent source agree, except where the card says it is the listing's claim; where sources
          conflicted, as on Speediance's subscription price, we say so on the card. Hardware and membership prices are those we saw on 19 October 2026
          and change often. We earn a commission if you buy through the Amazon links on this page; it
          does not change which products we list or what we say about them. <strong class="text-gray-900">What this page is, and is not.</strong> We review home exercise
          equipment; we are not giving medical, physiotherapy or coaching advice. Resistance figures
          are manufacturer maximums, not a guide to what you should lift. If you have a heart, joint or
          back condition, are pregnant or postpartum, or are returning from injury, talk to a qualified
          professional before starting or changing a strength programme. Wall mounting is a structural
          job: follow the maker's installation requirements, and if you rent, get the landlord's
          permission first.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#quick-picks">Our Picks at a Glance</a></li>
            <li><a href="#picks">The 5 Best Smart Home Gyms</a></li>
            <li><a href="#how-we-chose">How We Chose These Smart Gyms</a></li>
            <li><a href="#digital-resistance">How Digital Resistance Works, and What the Numbers Mean</a></li>
            <li><a href="#wall-vs-freestanding">Wall-Mounted vs Freestanding: What Your Room Needs</a></li>
            <li><a href="#membership-costs">What the Membership Really Costs</a></li>
            <li><a href="#buyers-guide">Smart Home Gym Buyer's Guide</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="quick-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Our Picks at a Glance</h2>
<p>
            Five systems, one for each common design. Most people choosing between the first two are
            really choosing between a wall and a patch of floor; the other three exist because a tighter
            budget, an existing power rack or a very small space changes the answer. Every pick links to
