        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Smart Gyms</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these systems. They were chosen from manufacturer product pages,
            installation requirements, warranty and membership terms, recall records and independent
            coverage, and the claims on this page are limited to what those sources support. Each pick
            had to be sold on Amazon US, and its basic figures — resistance range, size, warranty and the
            cost of the membership — had to agree between the maker and at least one independent
            reviewer. The one exception is the budget pick, the SQUATZ Apollo Board Mini: we found no
            independent coverage of it, so its card gives only what the seller's listing states.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Recall checks.</strong> We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall
            database by brand name, by manufacturer and in recall descriptions for every maker on this
            page — Tonal, Speediance and SQUATZ — and for the brands we considered and left out, including
            Beyond Power, Tempo, Amp, Vitruvian and <a href="/bowflex/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>BowFlex</a>. We found no recalls for Tonal, Speediance, SQUATZ or
            Beyond Power. The only Tempo-named recall on record is for kitchen pressure cookers sold by an
            unrelated company. BowFlex's recent recalls are its SelectTech adjustable dumbbells (June
            2025) and SelectTech barbells (2021), neither of which is a smart gym.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Tonal 1 Certified Refurbished, Beyond Power Voltra I and Tempo Move.</strong> All three
            were picks on this page until November 2026 and are still good products, but each is sold only
            from its maker's own store, not on Amazon. If you would rather buy direct, Tonal's refurbished
            first-generation unit was $2,495 (200 lb, same membership as the Tonal 2), the Voltra I was
            $2,199 plus a rack mount with no subscription fee, and Tempo Move's Starter Bundle was $708
            including the first year of training, all on 19 October 2026.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What we left out, and why</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Amp.</strong> Amp is a wall-mounted smart cable machine that uses your iPhone or iPad
            as its screen and has been covered favourably as a cheaper Tonal rival. In December 2025 the
            CPSC announced a recall of about 1,900 AMP MP2 Smart Fitness Machines, sold on Amp's website
            from January to August 2025 for about $1,800, because the arm can fail to lock and swing
            unexpectedly, posing a laceration and serious injury risk; owners were told to stop using it
            and book a free in-home repair. Amp's current pages do not make clear to us which hardware
            revision new orders receive, so it stays off the list. If you already own one, check the
            recall notice and confirm the repair has been done.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Vitruvian Trainer+.</strong> The Trainer+ is a floor-standing digital platform that
            often appears in Tonal comparisons. On 19
            October 2026 Vitruvian's own store listed only an accessory kit, and its home page carried a
            holding message rather than the machine, so we could not confirm it as currently sold.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>BowFlex.</strong> BowFlex's current home gyms, the Revolution (SpiraFlex resistance)
            and the Xtreme 2 SE (Power Rod resistance), are traditional machines without motors or
            connected coaching. They can be good buys, but they are not smart gyms; our <a href="/best-home-gym-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym machines guide</a> covers that category.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Newer Speediance machines.</strong> Speediance began selling the Gym Monster 3 and 3S
            in August 2026. Speediance lists the Gym Monster 3 at 220 lb and the 3S at 260 lb, but we found
            no independent coverage of them yet, so they are mentioned here rather than carded. The
            rack-mountable Gym Nano, launched in October 2026, now has independent coverage and is carded
            above.
            The Gym Monster 2 remains on sale alongside them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Fitness mirrors and app-only systems.</strong> Mirrors stream classes but add no
            resistance, and app-only coaching works with equipment you already own; neither is what most
            people mean by a smart home gym, so we left them out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="digital-resistance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Digital Resistance Works, and What the Numbers Mean</h2>
<p>
            All five picks use digital resistance: an electric motor winds a cable, and software
            sets how hard it pulls back. There is no stack of plates, so the load can change between reps,
            or within a rep, without you touching anything. That is the real advantage of the category,
            and it is why the quoted maximum is only part of the story.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Maximum load is split between arms</h3>
          <p>
            A machine with two arms or two cables usually quotes its total. Tonal's 250 lb is 125 lb per
            arm, and Speediance's 220 lb is 110 lb per side, as their reviewers describe. That matters for
            single-arm work and for movements where you hold both handles together: a single-arm row on a
            two-motor machine tops out at the per-side figure. The Gym Nano has one cable and quotes up to
            220 lb for it, so a single-cable movement can use the whole figure.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is 200 or 250 pounds enough?</h3>
          <p>
            For most people training for health and general strength, yes. Upper-body pulling and pressing
            movements, single-leg work and accessory lifts rarely need more. Where digital machines run out
            is the heavy two-legged lifts: a trained lifter's squat, deadlift or hip thrust can exceed what
            any of these produce, and the cable geometry of a wall unit is not the same as a barbell on
            your back. Garage Gym Reviews flags the maximum on both the Tonal and the Speediance as a limit for
            stronger users. If those lifts are central to your training, a <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">power rack</a> with a barbell is still the tool, and a rack-mounted unit like the Gym Nano can sit on it for
            cable work.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Modes beyond a constant weight</h3>
          <p>
            Because the motor controls the cable, digital machines offer modes a weight stack cannot:
            heavier lowering phases (eccentric loading), a band-like curve that gets harder as the cable
            extends, a "chain" mode that adds load toward the top of a lift, and spotter features that
            ease the weight when you slow down. Speediance lists Standard, Chain, Eccentric, Fixed Speed
            and Sled modes for its newest unit; rivals such as Beyond Power describe <a href="/best-resistance-bands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>resistance band</a> and damper
            modes; Tonal ties its smart features to its membership. These are genuinely
            useful for progression and for training alone, but they are software features, and on Tonal
            most of them sit behind the subscription.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Free weights with tracking</h3>
          <p>
            Tempo, which we no longer card because it is not sold on Amazon, takes the opposite approach. You lift ordinary colour-coded dumbbells and plates, and the
            Tempo app on an iPhone watches you through the phone's camera, counts reps, identifies the
            weight and gives form cues. There is no motor, so no adaptive load, but there is also nothing
            to fail electrically and nothing limiting you except the plates you own. The trade-off is the
            maximum: the Starter Bundle tops out at 50 lb, according to Garage Gym Reviews, and heavier
            packages cost more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wall-vs-freestanding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Wall-Mounted vs Freestanding: What Your Room Needs</h2>
<p>
            The choice between a wall unit and a floor unit is mostly decided by your walls, your floor and
            whether you own the home.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Wall-mounted (Tonal)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Studs or concrete.</strong> Tonal says the unit installs much like a TV bracket and
            works with most wood and metal studs 16 to 24 inches apart; our cable machine guide also notes
            installation onto solid concrete. Plaster-over-lath walls, unusual stud spacing and walls with
            pipes or wiring in the wrong place are things to raise before you order.</li>
            <li><strong>Height and floor.</strong> Tonal gives 7 ft 10 in as the ideal ceiling height for the
            arms to extend fully and says the unit can still work with a 7 ft ceiling. Our earlier check of
            Tonal and Garage Gym Reviews gave 7 ft of unobstructed wall and a 7 x 7 ft floor area in front.</li>
            <li><strong>Power and Wi-Fi.</strong> Tonal asks for continuous, reliable Wi-Fi and a grounded,
            three-prong outlet within 6 feet of the location, with no <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>extension cord</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Installation.</strong> Tonal's own team delivers and mounts the unit. On 19 October
            delivery and installation started at $295 depending on location, and Tonal listed a $300 fee to
            detach and reinstall in the same home.</li>
            <li><strong>Renting.</strong> Drilling into studs leaves holes, and a lease may forbid it. Ask the
            landlord in writing first, and keep the answer.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Freestanding (Speediance)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>No drilling.</strong> The Gym Monster 2 stands on its own base, and Speediance says it
            needs no drilling or wall mounting, which is why it is the usual answer for renters.</li>
            <li><strong>Floor space and weight.</strong> It occupies about 48 x 27 in unfolded and folds to
            about 14.5 in deep, but the unit weighs about 172 lb and PCWorld describes it arriving in three
            packages totalling just under 353 lb. Speediance sells a separate in-home delivery service,
            priced from $150 for ground-floor delivery on 19 October. Check that your floor and stairs suit it,
            and see our <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym flooring guide</a> if it is going on a hard floor.</li>
            <li><strong>Power.</strong> Like any motorised machine, plug it straight into a wall outlet; check
            Speediance's manual for its electrical requirements before choosing a spot.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Rack-mounted and portable (Gym Nano, SQUATZ)</h3>
          <p>
            The Gym Nano clamps to a power rack with the quick-release mount in the box, with other mounts
            sold separately, and runs on a rechargeable battery that Speediance rates at up to 8 hours. It
            needs a rack, or a mount, that can take the load you will pull. The SQUATZ Apollo Board Mini
            needs the least: per its listing it is a compact, foldable board you stand on, so all it asks
            for is a clear patch of floor and somewhere to store it. If you do not have a rack yet, our <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">squat rack guide</a> covers footprint and anchoring, and folding racks are a sibling guide in this series.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="membership-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What the Membership Really Costs</h2>
<p>
            The screen price is rarely the price. The table below adds three years of each system's
            membership to the hardware price we saw on 19 October 2026. It is arithmetic from those stated
            inputs, not a quote: it leaves out tax, financing, promotions and price changes, and the
            membership prices themselves change.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
