        <div id="buyers-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Smart Home Gym Buyer's Guide</h2>
<p>
            Work through these in order. Most regret with smart gyms comes from skipping one of the first
            three.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Decide whether you want a coach or a machine.</strong> If you mainly want guided
            programmes that change the weight for you, pay for the platform with the best content and
            accept the membership. If you mainly want cable resistance, a freestanding or rack-mounted
            unit without a mandatory fee, or an iron <a href="/best-cable-machines-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cable machine</a>,
            costs less over time.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the wall or the floor before the colour.</strong> For a wall unit, find the
            studs and measure the ceiling. For a freestanding unit, measure the unfolded footprint plus room
            to move, and plan the delivery route for a heavy crate.</li>
            <li><strong>Price three years, not one.</strong> Use the table above with current prices. A
            cheaper machine with a dearer membership can overtake a dearer machine within two years.</li>
            <li><strong>Match the maximum to your heaviest lift.</strong> Think about rows, squats and
            hinges, not curls, and remember that two-arm machines split their maximum between arms.</li>
            <li><strong>Read the warranty and the add-ons.</strong> Tonal and Speediance both include two
            years on the main unit, and Speediance gives the Gym Nano's battery and cable one year; a
            refurbished unit's warranty is whatever its listing states, so read it. Accessories you will need — handles, bars, benches,
            mounts — are often extra.</li>
            <li><strong>Think about a bench and a floor.</strong> Most programmes assume a bench. Our <a href="/best-weight-benches/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight bench guide</a> and <a href="/best-home-gym-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym accessories guide</a> cover both, and many people pair a smart unit with a set of <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">adjustable dumbbells</a> for carries and warm-ups.</li>
            <li><strong>Plan for cardio separately.</strong> None of these replaces a bike or treadmill for
            conditioning. If you want screen-led cardio too, our <a href="/best-peloton-alternatives/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton alternatives guide</a> and <a href="/best-cardio-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home cardio machines guide</a> pick up from here.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Smart Home Gym</h2>
          <p>Each of these is easy to avoid before you order and expensive to fix after installation.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Pricing the hardware and forgetting the membership</h3>
              <p>A smart gym with a required membership costs far more over three years than its sticker. Add the monthly fee for 36 months, the accessories the maker says you need, and delivery or installation, then compare that total with the alternatives. Do the sum with the prices on the day you buy, not the ones in a review.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ordering a wall unit before checking the wall</h3>
              <p>Wall-mounted machines need studs at the right spacing, enough ceiling height and an outlet nearby without an extension cord. Find the studs, measure the ceiling and check the outlet before you order, and get a landlord's permission in writing if you rent. A failed installation survey can delay delivery by weeks.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying for curls when your heaviest lift is a squat</h3>
              <p>A 200 or 250 lb maximum sounds like plenty until you split it between two arms and try to load a squat or hip thrust. Pick a system for your heaviest lower-body movement, or plan to keep a barbell and rack for those lifts and use the smart unit for everything else.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a used smart gym without a recall check</h3>
              <p>Secondhand smart gyms are common, and at least one recent model, the AMP MP2, has been recalled for an arm that can fail to lock. Get the model and serial number, search the CPSC recall database, and confirm with the maker that any required repair has been done and that the membership can transfer to you.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">If you can wall-mount and will use the coaching, buy the Tonal 2</strong>;
            if you cannot drill, or want to keep running costs down, the Speediance Gym Monster 2 is the
            better machine for you. A refurbished original Gym Monster brings digital resistance for less
            money, the Gym Nano makes a power rack smart, and the SQUATZ Apollo Board Mini is the small,
            cheap way in, bought on its listing's claims.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever you choose, price it over three years with the membership included, check the wall or
            floor before ordering, and keep a plan for the heavy lifts that 200 or 250 lb of cable cannot
            cover. If a coach on the wall is less important than a cable station that never needs a fee, <a href="/best-cable-machines-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">our cable machine guide</a> is the next place to look.
          </p>
        </div>

