        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best smart home gym?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most people who can wall-mount, the Tonal 2: 5 to 250 lb of digital resistance, camera form feedback and professional installation, at $4,295 plus a required $59.95-a-month membership. If you cannot drill, the freestanding Speediance Gym Monster 2 is the better choice, and its subscription is optional.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much does a smart home gym cost per month?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On 19 October 2026, Tonal's membership was $59.95 a month with a 12-month commitment; Speediance's optional Wellness+ was $19.99 a month or $149.99 a year on its store; Garage Gym Reviews lists $29 a month for the original Gym Monster; and Speediance's Gym Nano page described no subscription. Prices change, so check before buying.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you use Tonal without a membership?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only in a limited way. Tonal says each purchase requires a 12-month membership commitment and that an active membership is required for most features. If you want a digital machine that keeps most of its use without a subscription, the Speediance Gym Monster 2 or Gym Nano are better fits.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does a Tonal need to be mounted to studs?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Tonal says it installs much like a TV bracket on most wood and metal studs 16 to 24 inches apart, and its team does the installation. It also needs a grounded three-prong outlet within 6 feet, steady Wi-Fi, and ideally 7 feet 10 inches of ceiling, though Tonal says it can work with 7 feet.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best smart home gym for renters?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A freestanding or portable system. The Speediance Gym Monster 2 needs no drilling and folds against a wall, and the SQUATZ Apollo Board Mini is a small board you stand on. The Gym Nano needs a rack or mount rather than a wall. If you want a Tonal, get the landlord's written permission before installation.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a smart home gym worth it compared with weights?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is worth it if you will use the coaching and value the space saving and automatic load changes. It is poor value if you mainly want heavy squats and deadlifts, or will drop the membership after a few months. A rack, barbell and plates cost less over time and have no ceiling on load.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

